.0,212.7 562.4,215.7 563.7,218.8 565.1,221.9 566.4,225.0 567.7,228.1 568.9,231.3 570.2,234.4 571.5,237.6 572.7,240.8 573.9,244.0 575.2,247.2 576.4,250.5 577.6,253.8 578.7,257.0 579.9,260.3 581.1,263.6 582.2,267.0 583.3,270.3 584.4,273.7 585.6,277.0 586.6,280.4 587.7,283.8 588.8,287.2 589.9,290.7 590.9,294.1\\\" fill=\\\"none\\\" stroke=\\\"#e97050\\\" stroke-width=\\\"2\\\"/>\\n</svg>\"},{\"Table\":{\"headers\":[\"launch angle\",\"range (simulated)\"],\"rows\":[[\"0.2 rad\",\"130.4 m\"],[\"0.3 rad\",\"150.3 m\"],[\"0.4 rad\",\"161.7 m\"],[\"0.5 rad\",\"167.2 m\"],[\"0.6 rad\",\"168.1 m\"],[\"0.7 rad\",\"164.9 m\"],[\"0.8 rad\",\"158.1 m\"]]}}]"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_gap_md",
      "order": 5,
      "label": "How wrong is the formula?",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## How off is the vacuum-only formula, exactly?\n\nWith drag, range tops out near **0.570 rad (32.7°) at 168.3 m**, not at the vacuum-blessed 45°.\n\nSame muzzle speed, formula against simulation, head to head:",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 25
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_vacuum_gap",
      "order": 6,
      "label": "Formula vs simulation",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// The vacuum formula vs the simulator: same muzzle speed, same angles.\nlet mut rows = Vec::new();\n\nfor a in [0.2_f64, 0.35, 0.5, 0.65, 0.8] {\n    let vacuum = shared::V0 * shared::V0 * (2.0 * a).sin() / shared::G;\n    let sim = shared::range(a);\n    \n    rows.push(vec![\n        format!(\"{a:.2}\"),\n        format!(\"{vacuum:.1} m\"),\n        format!(\"{sim:.1} m\"),\n        format!(\"{:.1} m\", vacuum - sim),\n        format!(\"{:.1}%\", (vacuum - sim) / vacuum * 100.0),\n    ]);\n}\n\nTable::new(\n    vec![\"angle (rad)\", \"vacuum formula\", \"simulated with drag\", \"overshoot\", \"overshoot %\"],\n    rows,\n)",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "collapsed": true,
      "version": 28,
      "saved_output": "[{\"Table\":{\"headers\":[\"angle (rad)\",\"vacuum formula\",\"simulated with drag\",\"overshoot\",\"overshoot %\"],\"rows\":[[\"0.20\",\"397.0 m\",\"130.4 m\",\"266.6 m\",\"67.2%\"],[\"0.35\",\"656.7 m\",\"156.8 m\",\"499.9 m\",\"76.1%\"],[\"0.50\",\"857.8 m\",\"167.2 m\",\"690.6 m\",\"80.5%\"],[\"0.65\",\"982.2 m\",\"167.0 m\",\"815.3 m\",\"83.0%\"],[\"0.80\",\"1018.9 m\",\"158.1 m\",\"860.9 m\",\"84.5%\"]]}}]"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_euler_md",
      "order": 7,
      "label": "Euler and the step-size bill",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Euler integration\n\nThe integrator inside `range` is explicit Euler, the bluntest tool in numerical analysis: freeze the forces for one timestep, coast in a straight line, recompute. Each step commits an $O(dt^2)$ local error; over the roughly 500 steps of a flight these accumulate into an $O(dt)$ **global** error. First order: halve the step, halve the error.\n\nThe sweep below re-integrates one shot at step sizes from 160 ms down to 5 ms against a 0.1 ms reference. The error column reads 7.7 m, 3.8 m, 1.9 m, 0.95 m: each row half the one above, the first-order signature visible in raw digits.\n\nThe trade-off is the classic one: accuracy costs steps (30 at dt = 0.16, 989 at dt = 0.005). But there is a second bill that matters here: the reverse pass we are building toward walks **every step backwards**, and its stored state scales with the trip count. Your step size is also your derivative's memory budget.",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 26
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_dt_sweep",
      "order": 8,
      "label": "The step-size sweep",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Re-integrate the same shot at coarser and finer steps. Explicit Euler is\n// first-order: the error column should halve every time dt does.\nfn euler_range(angle: f64, dt: f64) -> (f64, u32) {\n    let (mut x, mut y) = (0.0_f64, 0.0_f64);\n    let (mut vx, mut vy) = (shared::V0 * angle.cos(), shared::V0 * angle.sin());\n    let mut steps = 0_u32;\n    loop {\n        let speed = (vx * vx + vy * vy).sqrt();\n        vx -= shared::DRAG * speed * vx * dt;\n        vy -= (shared::G + shared::DRAG * speed * vy) * dt;\n        let (nx, ny) = (x + vx * dt, y + vy * dt);\n        steps += 1;\n        if ny < 0.0 {\n            return (x + vx * dt * (y / (y - ny)), steps);\n        }\n        x = nx;\n        y = ny;\n    }\n}\n\nlet angle = 0.55_f64;\nlet (reference, _) = euler_range(angle, 0.0001);\n\nlet mut rows = Vec::new();\nfor dt in [0.16_f64, 0.08, 0.04, 0.02, 0.01, 0.005] {\n    let (r, steps) = euler_range(angle, dt);\n    rows.push(vec![\n        format!(\"{dt} s\"),\n        format!(\"{steps}\"),\n        format!(\"{r:.3} m\"),\n        format!(\"{:+.3} m\", r - reference),\n    ]);\n}\n\nTable::new(vec![\"dt\", \"steps\", \"range\", \"error vs dt = 0.0001\"], rows)",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "collapsed": true,
      "version": 24,
      "saved_output": "[{\"Table\":{\"headers\":[\"dt\",\"steps\",\"range\",\"error vs dt = 0.0001\"],\"rows\":[[\"0.16 s\",\"33\",\"147.721 m\",\"-21.756 m\"],[\"0.08 s\",\"70\",\"158.785 m\",\"-10.693 m\"],[\"0.04 s\",\"144\",\"164.178 m\",\"-5.299 m\"],[\"0.02 s\",\"291\",\"166.844 m\",\"-2.633 m\"],[\"0.01 s\",\"585\",\"168.170 m\",\"-1.308 m\"],[\"0.005 s\",\"1174\",\"168.831 m\",\"-0.647 m\"]]}}]"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_wall",
      "order": 9,
      "label": "No formula, no problem",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Two ways to get a derivative\n\n`range(angle)` exists only as a loop, and Newton's method (where this is headed) will demand $dR/d\\theta$. So where does a derivative come from when there is nothing to differentiate by hand?\n\nThe classical workaround is finite differences: nudge the angle, re-run the sim, divide. It works, but you pay a full simulation per probe and you inherit the [step-size error valley](/public/autodiff): truncation error fighting roundoff, best case ~1e-11, and a step size $h$ you must pick blind.\n\nEnzyme just _works_: it takes the **compiled** simulator, loop and all, and emits a second function that runs it *backwards*, accumulating exact adjoints. No $h$ to pick, no truncation term, no extra simulations per probe. The next cell puts the two side by side.",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 24
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_grad",
      "order": 10,
      "label": "Enzyme vs finite differences",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// d_range comes from the compiler: one attribute in the shared source.\nlet mut rows = Vec::new();\n\nfor a in [0.2_f64, 0.3, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8] {\n    let (r, enzyme) = shared::d_range(a, 1.0);\n    let h = 1e-5;\n    let fd = (shared::range(a + h) - shared::range(a - h)) / (2.0 * h);\n    \n    rows.push(vec![\n        format!(\"{a:.1}\"),\n        format!(\"{r:.3}\"),\n        format!(\"{enzyme:.4}\"),\n        format!(\"{fd:.4}\"),\n        format!(\"{:.1e}\", (enzyme - fd).abs()),\n    ]);\n}\n\nTable::new(\n    vec![\"angle (rad)\", \"range (m)\", \"dR/dθ (Enzyme)\", \"dR/dθ (finite diff)\", \"|Δ|\"],\n    rows,\n)",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "collapsed": true,
      "version": 26,
      "saved_output": "[{\"Table\":{\"headers\":[\"angle (rad)\",\"range (m)\",\"dR/dθ (Enzyme)\",\"dR/dθ (finite diff)\",\"|Δ|\"],\"rows\":[[\"0.2\",\"130.400\",\"257.5653\",\"257.5653\",\"1.1e-7\"],[\"0.3\",\"150.301\",\"149.9284\",\"149.9284\",\"1.1e-8\"],[\"0.4\",\"161.692\",\"81.9965\",\"81.9965\",\"4.2e-9\"],[\"0.5\",\"167.209\",\"30.5021\",\"30.5021\",\"4.1e-9\"],[\"0.6\",\"168.062\",\"-12.3459\",\"-12.3459\",\"2.1e-8\"],[\"0.7\",\"164.893\",\"-50.3817\",\"-50.3817\",\"2.1e-8\"],[\"0.8\",\"158.076\",\"-85.6359\",\"-85.6359\",\"2.8e-8\"]]}}]"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_adjoint_md",
      "order": 11,
      "label": "The trajectory, backwards",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Basically, the magic is in the rewind\n\n`d_range` does no symbolic algebra and nudges nothing. Reverse-mode AD generates a program with two halves. The **forward pass** runs the simulation exactly as written, recording what the second half will need (here, each step's velocities). The **reverse pass** starts at the landing point, seeds the sensitivity of the answer with respect to itself (exactly 1.0), and walks the loop *in reverse iteration order*, applying the transposed chain rule at every step.\n\nFor a mental model: the cannonball flies twice. The first flight is the physics, muzzle to crater. The second runs crater back to muzzle, and what flows along it is **influence** rather than position: at each timestep it carries the answer to \"if this velocity were perturbed, how much would the landing point move?\" When the reverse flight arrives at $t = 0$, all of that influence has landed on the launch angle, and that number *is* $dR/d\\theta$.\n\nThe loop's trip count is decided at runtime, and the reverse pass simply replays however many steps the tape recorded, 494 for this shot. One reverse sweep also prices the gradient for **any number of inputs**, which is why reverse mode underlies every neural network.\n\nThe next cell hand-rolls the scheme in about 35 lines and checks it against the compiler-generated `d_range`. If the adjoint picture is right, the two columns should agree far past anything finite differences can reach.",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 24
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_adjoint_hand",
      "order": 12,
      "label": "Hand-rolling the adjoint",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Enzyme's trick, hand-rolled once: tape the flight forward, then sweep the\n// tape BACKWARDS, dragging sensitivity from the crater to the muzzle.\nfn pull_back(vx: f64, vy: f64, s: f64, bvx: f64, bvy: f64) -> (f64, f64) {\n    // Transposed Jacobian of one velocity update (the chain rule, reversed).\n    let (k, dt) = (shared::DRAG, shared::DT);\n    let avx = bvx * (1.0 - k * dt * (s + vx * vx / s)) - bvy * k * dt * vy * vx / s;\n    let avy = bvy * (1.0 - k * dt * (s + vy * vy / s)) - bvx * k * dt * vx * vy / s;\n    (avx, avy)\n}\n\nlet theta = 0.55_f64;\nlet dt = shared::DT;\n\n// Forward pass: fly the ball, recording each step's incoming velocities.\nlet (mut x, mut y) = (0.0_f64, 0.0_f64);\nlet (mut vx, mut vy) = (shared::V0 * theta.cos(), shared::V0 * theta.sin());\nlet mut tape = Vec::new();\n\nlet (fy, fvx, fvy, hand_range) = loop {\n    let s = (vx * vx + vy * vy).sqrt();\n    tape.push((vx, vy, s));\n\n    let nvx = vx - shared::DRAG * s * vx * dt;\n    let nvy = vy - (shared::G + shared::DRAG * s * vy) * dt;\n\n    if y + nvy * dt < 0.0 {\n        // Landing interpolation simplifies to r = x - vx * y / vy.\n        break (y, nvx, nvy, x - nvx * y / nvy);\n    }\n\n    vx = nvx;\n    vy = nvy;\n    x += vx * dt;\n    y += vy * dt;\n};\n\n// Seed adjoints at the landing point, then pull the final (partial) step.\nlet (ax, ay) = (1.0_f64, -fvx / fvy);\nlet (lvx, lvy, ls) = tape.pop().unwrap();\nlet (mut avx, mut avy) = pull_back(lvx, lvy, ls, -fy / fvy, fvx * fy / (fvy * fvy));\n\n// Reverse pass: the whole trajectory, backwards, one adjoint per step.\nfor (pvx, pvy, ps) in tape.iter().rev() {\n    let (nx, ny) = pull_back(*pvx, *pvy, *ps, avx + ax * dt, avy + ay * dt);\n    \n    avx = nx;\n    avy = ny;\n}\n\n// Influence has arrived at t = 0; rotate it onto the launch angle.\nlet hand_grad = avx * (-shared::V0 * theta.sin()) + avy * (shared::V0 * theta.cos());\nlet (r, enzyme) = shared::d_range(theta, 1.0);\n\nTable::new(\n    vec![\"quantity\", \"hand-rolled tape\", \"compiler (d_range)\", \"|Δ|\"],\n    vec![\n        vec![\n            \"range (m)\".to_string(),\n            format!(\"{hand_range:.9}\"),\n            format!(\"{r:.9}\"),\n            format!(\"{:.1e}\", (hand_range - r).abs()),\n        ],\n        vec![\n            \"dR/dθ (m/rad)\".to_string(),\n            format!(\"{hand_grad:.9}\"),\n            format!(\"{enzyme:.9}\"),\n            format!(\"{:.1e}\", (hand_grad - enzyme).abs()),\n        ],\n    ],\n)",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "collapsed": true,
      "version": 29,
      "saved_output": "[{\"Table\":{\"headers\":[\"quantity\",\"hand-rolled tape\",\"compiler (d_range)\",\"|Δ|\"],\"rows\":[[\"range (m)\",\"168.169822720\",\"168.169822720\",\"0.0e0\"],[\"dR/dθ (m/rad)\",\"8.410415183\",\"8.410415183\",\"1.7e-13\"]]}}]"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_gotcha",
      "order": 13,
      "label": "The pothole worth knowing about",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Differentiating *events*\n\nThe first version of this simulator produced gradients that were **exactly right and completely useless**. Enzyme and finite differences agreed to four decimals, and both said the range was *decreasing* at angles where it was plainly increasing. Newton's method promptly launched the cannon into orbit.\n\nEnzyme was fine. The loop originally stopped at the **first whole timestep below ground**, so as the angle varied, the landing point jumped in 10 ms quanta. `range(angle)` was a microscopic sawtooth: globally rising, locally falling, and every derivative method faithfully reported the slope of the *teeth*.\n\nThe fix is one line, and it applies whenever you differentiate through a simulation with a stopping condition: **smooth the event**. Interpolate the final partial step to $y = 0$ (see the landing branch in the simulator cell up top) and the sawtooth collapses into the piecewise-smooth function the physics always described. **The attribute differentiates whatever you actually wrote. If your events are discontinuous, your gradients will be too.**",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 24
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_newton_md",
      "order": 14,
      "label": "Aiming as root-finding",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Aiming as root-finding\n\nWe want the angle that drops the ball on a target at 150 m, which is a root-finding problem: define $\\text{miss}(\\theta) = \\text{range}(\\theta) - 150$ and drive it to zero.\n\nNewton's method linearizes at the current guess and jumps to where the tangent line crosses zero: $\\theta \\leftarrow \\theta - \\text{miss}(\\theta)/\\text{miss}'(\\theta)$. Each iteration costs one forward flight plus one reverse flight (a single `d_range` call), so the whole aiming procedure below runs in about the budget of **eight plain simulations**. Because the derivative is exact, Newton's method delivers its famous **quadratic convergence**: near the root the error is squared every iteration, so the count of correct digits roughly doubles per step. A finite-difference slope would also converge here, but at two simulations per probe for a noisier slope; exactness is what makes the tables below so clean.",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 24
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_newton_tangents_md",
      "order": 15,
      "label": "Watching the tangent nudge",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Watching the tangent nudge the guess\n\nNewton's rule is easier to watch than to read. Plot $\\text{range}(\\theta)$ against launch angle, draw the target as a flat line, and at each guess draw the **tangent** to the curve: its slope is exactly $dR/d\\theta$, the number Enzyme returns. Where the tangent crosses the target is the next guess.\n\nDrag the slider to take the steps one at a time from $\\theta_0 = 0.15$. The miss falls **35 m → 8.5 m → 0.9 m → 0.01 m**, the count of correct digits roughly doubling each step: quadratic convergence, and by the fourth step the tangent lands on the root.",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 22
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_newton_slider",
      "order": 16,
      "label": "Iteration slider",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "ui::slider(\"newton_iter\", 0.0, 4.0)\n    .step(1.0)\n    .label(\"Newton iteration\")\n    .default_value(0.0)",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "collapsed": true,
      "version": 27,
      "saved_output": "[{\"Interactive\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"config\":\"{\\\"default\\\":0.0,\\\"label\\\":\\\"Newton iteration\\\",\\\"max\\\":4.0,\\\"min\\\":0.0,\\\"name\\\":\\\"newton_iter\\\",\\\"step\\\":1.0}\"}}]"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_newton_tangents",
      "order": 17,
      "label": "Newton, drawn",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Reads the \"newton_iter\" slider each frame and reveals Newton's tangents up\n// to that iteration. The derivative at each guess is the real Enzyme d_range.\nstruct NewtonAim {\n    curve: Vec<(f64, f64)>,\n    steps: Vec<(f64, f64, f64, f64)>,\n    root: f64,\n    a_range: (f64, f64),\n    y_range: (f64, f64),\n}\n\nimpl LiveView for NewtonAim {\n    fn init() -> Self {\n        let target = 150.0_f64;\n        let mut a = 0.15_f64;\n        let mut steps = Vec::new();\n        for _ in 0..8 {\n            let (r, dr) = shared::d_range(a, 1.0);\n            if (r - target).abs() < 1e-4 {\n                break;\n            }\n            let a_next = a - (r - target) / dr;\n            steps.push((a, r, a_next, shared::range(a_next)));\n            a = a_next;\n        }\n        let root = a;\n\n        let (a_lo, a_hi) = (0.10_f64, 0.38_f64);\n        let mut curve = Vec::new();\n        let (mut ymin, mut ymax) = (f64::INFINITY, f64::NEG_INFINITY);\n        for i in 0..=200 {\n            let ang = a_lo + (a_hi - a_lo) * i as f64 / 200.0;\n            let r = shared::range(ang);\n            ymin = ymin.min(r);\n            ymax = ymax.max(r);\n            curve.push((ang, r));\n        }\n        let pad = (ymax - ymin) * 0.1;\n        Self {\n            curve,\n            steps,\n            root,\n            a_range: (a_lo, a_hi),\n            y_range: (ymin - pad, ymax + pad),\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn tick(&mut self) -> LiveContent {\n        let v = sim::read::<f64>(\"newton_iter\").unwrap_or(0.0).round().max(0.0) as usize;\n        let svg = shared::plot_newton_tangents(\n            &self.curve,\n            &self.steps,\n            v,\n            150.0,\n            self.root,\n            self.a_range,\n            self.y_range,\n        );\n        LiveContent::Html(svg)\n    }\n\n    fn fps() -> u32 {\n        5\n    }\n}",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "collapsed": true,
      "version": 24,
      "saved_output": "[{\"LiveView\":{\"fps\":5,\"kind\":\"html\",\"content\":\"<svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 760 344\\\" xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" style=\\\"width:100%;background:#14141e;border-radius:6px;font-family:sans-serif\\\"><line x1=\\\"56.0\\\" y1=\\\"298.0\\\" x2=\\\"740.0\\\" y2=\\\"298.0\\\" stroke=\\\"#24242e\\\" stroke-width=\\\"1\\\"/><text x=\\\"50.0\\\" y=\\\"301.0\\\" fill=\\\"#6a6a80\\\" font-size=\\\"10\\\" text-anchor=\\\"end\\\">88</text><line x1=\\\"56.0\\\" y1=\\\"214.0\\\" x2=\\\"740.0\\\" y2=\\\"214.0\\\" stroke=\\\"#24242e\\\" stroke-width=\\\"1\\\"/><text x=\\\"50.0\\\" y=\\\"217.0\\\" fill=\\\"#6a6a80\\\" font-size=\\\"10\\\" text-anchor=\\\"end\\\">114</text><line x1=\\\"56.0\\\" y1=\\\"130.0\\\" x2=\\\"740.0\\\" y2=\\\"130.0\\\" stroke=\\\"#24242e\\\" stroke-width=\\\"1\\\"/><text x=\\\"50.0\\\" y=\\\"133.0\\\" fill=\\\"#6a6a80\\\" font-size=\\\"10\\\" text-anchor=\\\"end\\\">140</text><line x1=\\\"56.0\\\" y1=\\\"46.0\\\" x2=\\\"740.0\\\" y2=\\\"46.0\\\" stroke=\\\"#24242e\\\" stroke-width=\\\"1\\\"/><text x=\\\"50.0\\\" y=\\\"49.0\\\" fill=\\\"#6a6a80\\\" font-size=\\\"10\\\" text-anchor=\\\"end\\\">166</text><line x1=\\\"56.0\\\" y1=\\\"298.0\\\" x2=\\\"56.0\\\" y2=\\\"303.0\\\" stroke=\\\"#3a3a4e\\\" stroke-width=\\\"1\\\"/><text x=\\\"56.0\\\" y=\\\"316.0\\\" fill=\\\"#6a6a80\\\" font-size=\\\"10\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">0.10</text><line x1=\\\"178.1\\\" y1=\\\"298.0\\\" x2=\\\"178.1\\\" y2=\\\"303.0\\\" stroke=\\\"#3a3a4e\\\" stroke-width=\\\"1\\\"/><text x=\\\"178.1\\\" y=\\\"316.0\\\" fill=\\\"#6a6a80\\\" font-size=\\\"10\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">0.15</text><line x1=\\\"300.3\\\" y1=\\\"298.0\\\" x2=\\\"300.3\\\" y2=\\\"303.0\\\" stroke=\\\"#3a3a4e\\\" stroke-width=\\\"1\\\"/><text x=\\\"300.3\\\" y=\\\"316.0\\\" fill=\\\"#6a6a80\\\" font-size=\\\"10\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">0.20</text><line x1=\\\"422.4\\\" y1=\\\"298.0\\\" x2=\\\"422.4\\\" y2=\\\"303.0\\\" stroke=\\\"#3a3a4e\\\" stroke-width=\\\"1\\\