    {
      "id": "cell_digits_md",
      "order": 20,
      "label": "Read the digits",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Read the digits\n\nThe table above stops when the miss drops under 0.1 mm, which undersells how good the convergence is. Below is the same iteration with the miss in scientific notation, plus the ratio $|\\text{miss}_n| / |\\text{miss}_{n-1}|^2$. Quadratic convergence predicts that ratio is roughly *constant* (it is the curvature-to-slope ratio at the root), and the exponent staircase runs **1e+1, 1e+0, 1e-2, 1e-7, 1e-13**, correct digits doubling once the iteration gets close, with the ratio column parked near 0.005–0.014 the whole way down.",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 23
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_newton_digits",
      "order": 21,
      "label": "Quadratic convergence table",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// The same Newton iteration, instrumented for its convergence RATE. If the\n// convergence is quadratic, |miss_n| / |miss_(n-1)|^2 is roughly constant.\nlet target = 150.0_f64;\nlet mut angle = 0.15_f64;\nlet mut prev: Option<f64> = None;\nlet mut rows = Vec::new();\n\nfor i in 0..6 {\n    let (r, dr) = shared::d_range(angle, 1.0);\n    let miss = (r - target).abs();\n    let ratio = prev.map_or(\"-\".to_string(), |p| format!(\"{:.4}\", miss / (p * p)));\n\n    rows.push(vec![format!(\"{i}\"), format!(\"{miss:.3e}\"), ratio]);\n\n    if miss < 1e-10 {\n        break;\n    }\n    \n    prev = Some(miss);\n    angle -= (r - target) / dr;\n}\n\nTable::new(vec![\"iteration\", \"|miss| (m)\", \"|miss| / previous²\"], rows)",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "collapsed": true,
      "version": 27,
      "saved_output": "[{\"Table\":{\"headers\":[\"iteration\",\"|miss| (m)\",\"|miss| / previous²\"],\"rows\":[[\"0\",\"3.463e1\",\"-\"],[\"1\",\"8.482e0\",\"0.0071\"],[\"2\",\"8.919e-1\",\"0.0124\"],[\"3\",\"1.117e-2\",\"0.0140\"],[\"4\",\"6.619e-7\",\"0.0053\"],[\"5\",\"1.137e-13\",\"0.2595\"]]}}]"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_two_md",
      "order": 22,
      "label": "Two solutions",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Two ways to hit the same target\n\n`range(θ)` climbs to its 168.3 m maximum at 0.570 rad and then falls, so every reachable target is crossed **twice**: once on the way up (a flat shot) and once on the way down (a lofted one). For the 150 m target, the roots sit near 17.1° and 50.5°.\n\nNewton is a local algorithm: it converges to whichever root's basin you start in. Start at 0.15 rad and you get the flat shot. Start at 1.2 rad, where $dR/d\\theta$ is negative, and the identical update rule marches the other way and finds the mortar solution. Same crater, wildly different flights: apex 18 m vs 80 m, hang time 3.8 s vs 7.9 s. Artillery doctrine has names for the two (direct and indirect fire); the optimizer rediscovers the distinction from nothing but a sign.\n\nOn the crest, $dR/d\\theta$ passes through zero (at 0.570 rad), so a start there makes Newton divide by almost nothing and catapult the guess far away; that is where you would add a bracket or a line search. From sensible starts, a handful of iterations land either shot.",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 23
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_two_solutions",
      "order": 23,
      "label": "Flat shot vs mortar shot",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Two roots, one target: start Newton in different basins and it finds\n// different shots. Everything here flows from the derivative's sign.\nfn aim(mut angle: f64, target: f64) -> (f64, usize) {\n    for i in 0..12 {\n        let (r, dr) = shared::d_range(angle, 1.0);\n        if (r - target).abs() < 1e-9 {\n            return (angle, i);\n        }\n        angle -= (r - target) / dr;\n    }\n    (angle, 12)\n}\n\nlet target = 150.0_f64;\nlet (flat, flat_iters) = aim(0.15, target);\nlet (lofted, lofted_iters) = aim(1.2, target);\n\nlet mut rows = Vec::new();\nfor (name, a, iters) in [(\"flat\", flat, flat_iters), (\"lofted\", lofted, lofted_iters)] {\n    let pts = shared::trajectory(a);\n    let apex = pts.iter().map(|p| p.1).fold(0.0_f64, f64::max);\n    let hang = (pts.len() - 1) as f64 * shared::DT;\n\n    rows.push(vec![\n        name.to_string(),\n        format!(\"{a:.4} rad ({:.1}°)\", a.to_degrees()),\n        format!(\"{:.3} m\", shared::range(a)),\n        format!(\"{apex:.1} m\"),\n        format!(\"{hang:.2} s\"),\n        format!(\"{iters}\"),\n    ]);\n}\n\nTable::new(\n    vec![\"shot\", \"angle\", \"range\", \"apex\", \"hang time\", \"Newton iterations\"],\n    rows,\n)",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "collapsed": true,
      "version": 28,
      "saved_output": "[{\"Table\":{\"headers\":[\"shot\",\"angle\",\"range\",\"apex\",\"hang time\",\"Newton iterations\"],\"rows\":[[\"flat\",\"0.2980 rad (17.1°)\",\"150.000 m\",\"18.5 m\",\"3.78 s\",\"5\"],[\"lofted\",\"0.8814 rad (50.5°)\",\"150.000 m\",\"79.7 m\",\"7.95 s\",\"5\"]]}}]"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_basins_md",
      "order": 24,
      "label": "The miss landscape",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## The Newton troughs visualized\n\nThe two solutions are clearest as a picture. Plot the miss, $|\\text{range}(\\theta) - 150|$, against launch angle and it falls into a **double well**: zero at the flat shot, zero at the lofted shot, with an overshoot hump between them where the crest carries the ball past 150 m before drag reins it back in.\n\nThe shading answers the sequel question by brute force: from every starting angle, run Newton to convergence and color that column by the trough it fell into. The split lands right on the crest, where $dR/d\\theta = 0$ and the update briefly divides by almost nothing and flings the guess out of the physical range (the gray seam). Start at 0.15 rad and you roll into the flat trough; start at 1.2 and the identical rule catches the lofted one.",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "version": 23
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_basins",
      "order": 25,
      "label": "Two troughs, one target",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// The miss landscape: |range(θ) − target| against launch angle. The two\n// troughs are the two firing solutions; shade each starting angle by the trough\n// Newton rolls into from there (brute force, one Newton run per column).\nlet target = 150.0_f64;\nlet (a_lo, a_hi, n) = (0.10_f64, 1.48_f64, 260usize);\n\n// The two solutions, from the notebook's two basins.\nlet mut flat = 0.15_f64;\nfor _ in 0..50 {\n    let (r, d) = shared::d_range(flat, 1.0);\n    flat -= (r - target) / d;\n}\n\nlet mut lofted = 1.2_f64;\nfor _ in 0..50 {\n    let (r, d) = shared::d_range(lofted, 1.0);\n    lofted -= (r - target) / d;\n}\n\n// The range crest is the watershed between the basins.\nlet mut crest = a_lo;\nlet mut best = shared::range(a_lo);\n\nfor i in 0..=400 {\n    let a = a_lo + (a_hi - a_lo) * i as f64 / 400.0;\n    let r = shared::range(a);\n    if r > best {\n        best = r;\n        crest = a;\n    }\n}\n\n// Which trough does Newton reach from a given start? (2 = flung off near the crest.)\nlet classify = |mut a: f64| -> u8 {\n    for _ in 0..40 {\n        let (rng, dr) = shared::d_range(a, 1.0);\n        if dr.abs() < 1e-9 {\n            return 2;\n        }\n        a -= (rng - target) / dr;\n        if !a.is_finite() || a <= 0.0 || a >= std::f64::consts::FRAC_PI_2 {\n            return 2;\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (a - flat).abs() < 1e-3 {\n        0\n    } else if (a - lofted).abs() < 1e-3 {\n        1\n    } else {\n        2\n    }\n};\n\n// Sample the miss curve and the basin under each angle.\nlet mut curve = Vec::with_capacity(n + 1);\nlet mut y_max = 0.0_f64;\n\nfor i in 0..=n {\n    let a = a_lo + (a_hi - a_lo) * i as f64 / n as f64;\n    let miss = (shared::range(a) - target).abs();\n\n    if miss > y_max {\n        y_max = miss;\n    }\n    curve.push((a, miss, classify(a)));\n}\n\nlet y_max = y_max * 1.04;\n\nlet roots = vec![\n    (flat, format!(\"flat  {:.1}°\", flat.to_degrees())),\n    (lofted, format!(\"lofted  {:.1}°\", lofted.to_degrees())),\n];\n\nlet starts = vec![\n    (0.15_f64, flat, \"start 0.15\".to_string()),\n    (1.20_f64, lofted, \"start 1.2\".to_string()),\n];\n\nSvg(shared::plot_miss_basins(&curve, &roots, &starts, crest, y_max))",
      "cargo_toml": "",
      "collapsed": true,
      "version": 33,
      "saved_output": "[{\"Svg\":\"<svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 760 340\\\" xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" style=\\\"width:100%;background:#14141e;border-radius:6px;font-family:sans-serif\\\"><rect x=\\\"54.0\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"56.6\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"59.3\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"61.9\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"64.6\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"67.2\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"69.9\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"72.5\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"75.2\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"77.8\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"80.5\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"83.1\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"85.8\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"88.4\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"91.0\\\" y=\\\"26.0\\\" width=\\\"3.35\\\" height=\\\"268.0\\\" fill=\\\"#4fc3f7\\\" opacity=\\\"0.14\\\"/><rect x=\\\"93.7\\\" 